      "line": [
        "Nc6"
      ],
      "spoken": "The knight to c6 is powerful, because your opponent is almost certainly rolling his eyes.",
      "reanchor": true
    },
    {
      "ply": 9,
      "t": 620.5,
      "fen": "r1bqkb1r/ppp1pppp/2n2n2/6N1/4P3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 0 5",
      "line": [
        "dxe4"
      ],
      "spoken": "Objectively he should probably play d takes e4.",
      "reanchor": true
    },
    {
      "ply": 13,
      "t": 626,
      "fen": "r1b1kb1r/ppp1pppp/2n5/8/4N3/8/PPP2PPP/RNBK1B1R b kq - 0 7",
      "line": [
        "Qxd1+",
        "Kxd1",
        "Nxe4",
        "Nxe4"
      ],
      "spoken": "The queens come off on d1, the king recaptures losing castling rights, and the knights trade — Tennyson is another name for it, but no gambit player wants this position.",
      "reanchor": true
    },
    {
      "ply": 8,
      "t": 649,
      "fen": "r1bqkb1r/ppp1pppp/2n2n2/6N1/4p3/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 1 5",
      "line": [],
      "spoken": "From a practical standpoint, nobody wants to play this — a good example of harnessing your opponent's psychology.",
      "reanchor": true
    },
    {
      "ply": 7,
      "t": 654.5,
      "fen": "rnbqkb1r/ppp1pppp/5n2/6N1/4p3/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R b KQkq - 0 4",
      "line": [],
      "spoken": "But again, don't take this too far.",
      "reanchor": true
    },
    {
      "ply": 8,
      "t": 657,
      "fen": "r1bqkb1r/ppp1pppp/2n2n2/6N1/4p3/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 1 5",
      "line": [
        "Nc6"
      ],
      "spoken": "The knight to c6.",
